    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "32-35",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Jays are stuck in that ugly middle ground where every loss feels heavier than the one before it. <strong>Monday night's 5-2 defeat to the Phillies</strong> dropped Toronto to 32-35, left it 3rd in the AL East, and kept the club a game under water with an 8-game gap to make up; that is not contender oxygen, it is survival mode. The offense never solved Cristopher Sánchez, who punched out 10 over seven, while Erik Clemente's homer provided only a brief pulse. On the bright side, the rotation at least has a lifeline: Dylan Cease is expected to come off the injured list Tuesday to start against Zack Wheeler, and that gives Toronto a legitimate arm to steady a staff that just watched Paul Corbin get chased after a rough three-inning night. The Jays have split their last three against Baltimore before the Phillies knocked them back again, so the next turn matters. <strong>Cease's return against Wheeler is the kind of reset game that can tell you whether this club still has a pulse</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated June 9, 2026"
      },
